# Quickstart

This page starts the generated service locally and exercises its two pristine HTTP endpoints.

Complete `docs/getting-started/installation.md`, provide valid PostgreSQL and required base
operation settings in `.env`, and start the development server:

```bash
make run
```

The server listens on the configured `http.host` and `http.port`; the example `.env` uses `127.0.0.1:8000`.

## Service root

Call the service root path:

```bash
curl --fail-with-body http://127.0.0.1:8000/
```

The response is the exact successful `ApplicationResponse[None]` envelope:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "result": null,
  "error": null,
  "error_message": null
}
```

## OpenAPI document

Retrieve the public OpenAPI document through its system operation path:

```bash
curl --fail-with-body http://127.0.0.1:8000/openapi.get
```

That path reflects the `.env.example` value `OPENAPI_OPERATION=openapi.get`. If configuration uses
`contract.getOpenAPI`, request `/contract.getOpenAPI` instead.

The response uses the same four-field envelope. Its `result` is the complete FastAPI-generated OpenAPI object; the object includes the following stable service metadata:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "result": {
    "openapi": "3.1.0",
    "info": {
      "title": "{{ service_name }}",
      "version": "0.1.0"
    },
    "x-pokerlost-service": {
      "id": "{{ service_name }}"
    }
  },
  "error": null,
  "error_message": null
}
```

The displayed `result` focuses on stable metadata; the actual object also contains the generated
`paths` and schemas. See `docs/api/openapi-and-sdk.md` for publication rules and
`docs/api/public-surface.md` for the exact pristine endpoints.
